<!-- §01 ─ The Speed–Recall Trade-off ──────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-tradeoff" data-code="tradeoff">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Speed–Recall Trade-off</div>
<h2>Attention is great at retrieval. Linear models are fast. You usually have to pick one.</h2>
<p>
  Softmax attention reads every previous token on every step. It never forgets — but that comes at a cost: the KV cache grows linearly with sequence length, and computing attention takes O(n²) work. As sequences grow to thousands of tokens, this becomes expensive.
</p>
<p>
  Linear recurrent models like Mamba-2 and Gated DeltaNet (GDN) compress the entire past into a fixed-size state matrix. Each step is O(1) memory, O(n) total — fast, cache-free. But that compression loses information. Ask them to find a specific fact buried 1,500 tokens ago and they often fail.
</p>
<p>
  The chart below makes this concrete. On <strong>Needle-in-a-Haystack (NIAH-3)</strong> — the hardest variant, where the model must retrieve a specific fact from a 2K-token context — Mamba-2 succeeds just 33% of the time. Softmax attention succeeds 95% of the time.
</p>

<div class="vp" id="vp-niah">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">needle-in-a-haystack · NIAH-3 · 1.4B models · 2K context</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">the retrieval gap at a glance</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="niah-canvas" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  Most real tasks aren't uniformly retrieval-heavy. A reasoning trace might spend 1,000 tokens on working memory and just 50 tokens retrieving a named fact. Wouldn't it be great if the model could use the cheap linear mode for the former and switch to attention only when it needs to look something up?
</p>
<p>
  Existing hybrid architectures don't offer this flexibility. <strong>Inter-layer hybrids</strong> (e.g., Jamba, NVIDIA Hymba) fix attention at certain layers and linear recurrence at others — the assignment is static and layer-level. <strong>Intra-layer hybrids</strong> blend both mechanisms within a single block. Neither approach lets you vary the mechanism <em>along the sequence</em>.
</p>
<p>
  Oryx introduces a third option: <strong>sequence-axis hybridization</strong>. The model can switch between softmax attention and a linear recurrent mechanism from chunk to chunk, dynamically allocating richer context utilization exactly where it's needed.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 ─ One Language for Both ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-shared" data-code="shared">
<div class="section-no">02 — One Language for Both</div>
<h2>Attention and linear RNNs both speak Query–Key–Value</h2>
<p>
  The key observation that makes Oryx possible is that softmax attention and modern linear RNNs can be viewed through the same lens: <strong>associative key-value memory</strong>. Both mechanisms project the input into queries, keys, and values using learned weight matrices, and both produce outputs by querying a stored memory with the current query vector.
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Input → Shared Projections</div>
  <span class="math-var">q</span> = x · W<sub>Q</sub>,&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-var">k</span> = x · W<sub>K</sub>,&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-var">v</span> = x · W<sub>V</sub>
</div>

<p>
  They differ only in <em>how</em> they store and query this memory:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Softmax attention</strong> keeps an explicit, ever-growing KV cache. At each step it computes a softmax-weighted sum over all past (k, v) pairs.</li>
  <li><strong>Mamba-2 / GDN</strong> compress the past into a fixed-size state matrix S. Each new (k, v) pair updates S via an outer product; the output is a simple query read-out: o = q · S.</li>
</ul>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">associative memory view · both mechanisms use Q, K, V</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">same projections, different memory storage</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <svg id="kv-svg" viewBox="0 0 640 240" style="width:100%;max-width:640px;display:block;margin:0 auto" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
      <!-- Input token -->
      <rect x="265" y="8" width="110" height="36" rx="6" fill="rgba(139,92,246,.18)" stroke="rgba(139,92,246,.5)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
      <text x="320" y="31" text-anchor="middle" fill="#c4b5fd" font-size="12" font-family="monospace" font-weight="600">input token x</text>
      <!-- Arrow down -->
      <line x1="320" y1="44" x2="320" y2="68" stroke="rgba(255,255,255,.2)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arr)"/>
      <!-- Shared projections box -->
      <rect x="200" y="68" width="240" height="36" rx="6" fill="rgba(245,158,11,.12)" stroke="rgba(245,158,11,.45)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
      <text x="320" y="82" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fbbf24" font-size="10" font-family="monospace">SHARED K, V projections</text>
      <text x="320" y="96" text-anchor="middle" fill="#92400e" font-size="9" font-family="monospace">k = xW_K,  v = xW_V  (tied across modes)</text>
      <!-- Arrow left and right -->
      <line x1="250" y1="104" x2="140" y2="132" stroke="rgba(255,255,255,.2)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arr)"/>
      <line x1="390" y1="104" x2="500" y2="132" stroke="rgba(255,255,255,.2)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arr)"/>
      <!-- Attention box -->
      <rect x="40" y="132" width="190" height="60" rx="6" fill="rgba(139,92,246,.12)" stroke="rgba(139,92,246,.4)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
      <text x="135" y="152" text-anchor="middle" fill="#c4b5fd" font-size="11" font-family="monospace" font-weight="600">Softmax Attention</text>
      <text x="135" y="168" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7c3aed" font-size="9" font-family="monospace">KV cache: grows with t</text>
      <text x="135" y="181" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7c3aed" font-size="9" font-family="monospace">o = softmax(q K^T) V</text>
      <!-- Linear box -->
      <rect x="410" y="132" width="190" height="60" rx="6" fill="rgba(6,182,212,.1)" stroke="rgba(6,182,212,.35)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
      <text x="505" y="152" text-anchor="middle" fill="#67e8f9" font-size="11" font-family="monospace" font-weight="600">Linear Recurrence</text>
      <text x="505" y="168" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0e7490" font-size="9" font-family="monospace">state S: fixed size</text>
      <text x="505" y="181" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0e7490" font-size="9" font-family="monospace">S ← A·S + k^T·v; o = q·S</text>
      <!-- Separate Q boxes -->
      <rect x="40" y="202" width="190" height="30" rx="5" fill="rgba(16,185,129,.1)" stroke="rgba(16,185,129,.3)" stroke-width="1"/>
      <text x="135" y="221" text-anchor="middle" fill="#6ee7b7" font-size="9" font-family="monospace">Q_attn  (separate weights)</text>
      <rect x="410" y="202" width="190" height="30" rx="5" fill="rgba(16,185,129,.1)" stroke="rgba(16,185,129,.3)" stroke-width="1"/>
      <text x="505" y="221" text-anchor="middle" fill="#6ee7b7" font-size="9" font-family="monospace">Q_linear  (separate weights)</text>

      <defs>
        <marker id="arr" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" refX="4" refY="4" orient="auto">
          <path d="M0,0 L0,8 L8,4 z" fill="rgba(255,255,255,.25)"/>
        </marker>
      </defs>
    </svg>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The shared K and V projections are the linchpin. If both mechanisms derive their keys and values from the same weight matrices, then updating one mechanism's state is equivalent to updating the other's — they're accumulating the same associations in different storage formats. This compatibility is what allows switching without information loss.
</p>
<p>
  The query projections, however, are kept <em>separate</em> per mechanism. This matters: the query is the read-out operation, and the way you want to retrieve information from a softmax KV cache differs from how you read out a compressed linear state. Sharing queries hurts performance — an ablation confirms it.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §03 ─ The Oryx Block ────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-block" data-code="block">
<div class="section-no">03 — The Oryx Block</div>
<h2>One block, two modes, 90%+ shared weights</h2>
<p>
  The Oryx block wraps both mechanisms in a unified layer. At each forward pass, a <strong>mode</strong> is selected (attention or linear), but both internal states are updated regardless — keeping them synchronized so the model can switch cleanly on the next chunk.
</p>

