</div>

<h3>What's shared vs. unique</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Shared (amber)</strong>: K projection W_K, V projection W_V, gate W_G, output projection W_O, short convolution, all MLP weights — more than 90% of total parameters</li>
  <li><strong>Unique (green)</strong>: Query projection W_Q per mechanism (attention vs linear) — different readout strategies from different state representations</li>
  <li><strong>Both updated always</strong>: Both the KV cache and the linear RNN state receive updates every token step, keeping them in sync even when their output is not selected</li>
</ul>

<p>
  Because both states are always kept current, switching the active mixer on the next chunk doesn't require any state re-computation. The selected mixer simply continues reading from a state that has already been accumulating compatible representations.
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Oryx block forward (single token, mode M selected)</div>
  q_M = x · W_Q_M<span class="math-comment">  &lt;-- mode-specific query</span>
  k = conv(x · W_K),  v = conv(x · W_V)<span class="math-comment">  &lt;-- shared</span>
  KV_cache ← append(k, v)<span class="math-comment">            &lt;-- always update</span>
  S_t = A_t · S_&#123;t-1&#125; + k^T · v<span class="math-comment">       &lt;-- always update</span>
  o = Mixer_M(q_M, KV_cache or S_t)<span class="math-comment">  &lt;-- select output</span>
  Y = GatedRMSNorm(o, σ(x·W_G)) · W_O<span class="math-comment">  &lt;-- shared</span>
</div>
</section>

<!-- §04 ─ Chunked Mixed-Mode Training ───────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-training" data-code="training">
<div class="section-no">04 — Chunked Mixed-Mode Training</div>
<h2>Train with random chunk assignments to teach seamless switching</h2>
<p>
  Sharing weights is necessary but not sufficient for robust mode switching. If the model is only ever trained with one mechanism active for the entire sequence, the two modes learn slightly incompatible representations and switching mid-sequence causes a perplexity spike that takes a long time to recover.
</p>
<p>
  The solution is <strong>chunked mixed-mode training</strong>. Each training sequence is divided into fixed-length chunks of 128 tokens. Each chunk is independently and randomly assigned to either softmax attention or the linear mode, at a <strong>1:3 attention-to-linear ratio</strong>. All Oryx blocks in the model use the same chunk assignment for a given sequence.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">chunked mixed-mode training · 16 chunks · 128 tokens each</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click Randomize to see a new training assignment</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="chunk-controls">
      <button class="chunk-btn" id="chunk-randomize">Randomize</button>
      <span class="chunk-stat" id="chunk-stat">attn: <span id="attn-count">4</span> &nbsp;|&nbsp; linear: <span id="linear-count">12</span></span>
    </div>
    <canvas id="chunk-canvas" style="width:100%;height:100px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  This forces the model to learn representations that are compatible at chunk boundaries in every possible configuration. After chunked training, the internal representations of both modes are aligned — keys and values encode the same semantic structure regardless of which mechanism is active.
</p>

<h3>Why 1:3?</h3>
<p>
  The ratio balances the performance of both modes. More attention chunks improve retrieval but slow training; more linear chunks preserve efficiency but may weaken attention-mode generalization. Ablations show 1:3 is the sweet spot.
</p>
<p>
  Note that the model is effectively never trained with all chunks assigned to the same mode — the probability that all 16 chunks pick attention is (1/4)¹⁶ ≈ 2.3×10⁻¹⁰. Yet it generalizes perfectly to single-mode deployment.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §05 ─ Mode Switching Just Works ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-switch" data-code="switch">
<div class="section-no">05 — Mode Switching Just Works</div>
<h2>After a switch, perplexity recovers in ~200 tokens</h2>
<p>
  The real test is what happens at inference when you actually switch mechanisms mid-sequence. If the representations aren't compatible, perplexity should spike and stay high after the switch. If they are compatible, it should spike briefly and recover quickly.
</p>
<p>
  The chart below shows token-level perplexity for the 1.4B Oryx-TM model (Transformer / Mamba-2 variant). You can switch between the two directions and pick the switch point. The dashed lines show the no-switch baselines for reference.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">token-level perplexity after mixer switch · 1.4B Oryx-TM</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">select direction and switch position</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="ppl-controls">
      <div class="ppl-dir-toggle">
        <button class="ppl-dir-btn active" data-dir="attn2mamba">Attn → Mamba-2</button>
        <button class="ppl-dir-btn" data-dir="mamba2attn">Mamba-2 → Attn</button>
      </div>
      <div class="ppl-switch-btns">
        <button class="ppl-sw-btn active" data-sw="512">Switch @512</button>
        <button class="ppl-sw-btn" data-sw="1024">Switch @1024</button>
        <button class="ppl-sw-btn" data-sw="1536">Switch @1536</button>
        <button class="ppl-sw-btn" data-sw="none">No Switch</button>
      </div>
    </div>
    <canvas id="ppl-canvas" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  In every case the perplexity spike resolves within roughly 150–200 tokens, after which the model runs at the same perplexity as the baseline that never switched. This holds for both directions and all switch positions.
</p>
<p>
  By contrast, models trained without chunked mixed-mode training (sequence-level assignment only) show a much more severe spike when switching from Mamba-2 to attention — in some configurations it never fully recovers.
</p>

<h3>What this unlocks at inference time</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Linear prefill, attention generation</strong>: Process the long context with the cheap linear mode (O(n) compute, constant memory), then switch to attention for the query + answer where retrieval matters most.</li>
  <li><strong>Per-task routing</strong>: Route to attention for retrieval-heavy requests (Q&A, fact lookup), linear for generation-heavy ones (summarization, continuation).</li>
  <li><strong>Speculative execution</strong>: Draft a reasoning trace with the linear model, verify / finalize with attention.</li>
</ul>
</section>

